Instruction: Read the following segment of an acquisition agreement and answer the multiple-choice question by choosing the option that best characterizes the agreement.

Question: Is “financial point of view” the sole consideration when determining whether an offer is superior?
Option A: No
Option B: Yes

Acquisition Agreement:
SECTION 9.2 Evaluation of Superior Proposals.
(a) In order for any Acquisition Proposal to be classified as a Superior Proposal, the Company Board must conduct a comprehensive evaluation that is not limited solely to the financial terms offered. 
(b) The evaluation process shall include, but not be limited to, the following criteria:
    (i) A thorough analysis of the financial consideration, including cash, stock, or other financial instruments; 
    (ii) An assessment of strategic fit and potential operational synergies with the Company’s long-term goals; 
    (iii) Mandatory consultation with external legal counsel and a financial advisor of recognized national standing to ensure that all fiduciary duties under applicable law are duly observed;
    (iv) Engagement in a minimum three (3) Business Day notice period during which the Board is required to negotiate in good faith with Parent representatives regarding any proposed modifications to the terms of the existing agreement;
    (v) An evaluation of any non-financial factors such as risk allocation, contractual obligations, and regulatory compliance issues.
(c) The failure to meet any one of the criteria listed above may result in the acquisition proposal being disqualified as a Superior Proposal, regardless of the attractiveness of the financial terms.

Please provide your answer.
