Instruction: Read the following segment of an Acquisition Agreement and answer the multiple-choice question by choosing the option that best characterizes the evaluation process for a Superior Proposal.

Question: Based on the Agreement excerpt provided, is the 'financial point of view' the sole consideration when determining whether an offer is a Superior Proposal?
Option A: No
Option B: Yes

Acquisition Agreement: Section 8.3 – Superior Proposal Determination. (a) Upon receipt of any unsolicited acquisition proposal, the Board of Directors shall evaluate the offer based on its aggregate merits. This evaluation shall include, but is not limited to:
      (i) a thorough financial analysis conducted by a nationally recognized financial consultant;
      (ii) a strategic assessment performed in consultation with the Company’s strategic advisor and market research group;
      (iii) an evaluation of regulatory implications and compliance risks, requiring input from the Company’s legal counsel and compliance officer;
      (iv) a review of potential impacts on corporate governance and shareholder value, to be determined after discussions with financial, strategic, and independent industry experts.
(b) The Board's conclusion regarding whether an acquisition proposal qualifies as a Superior Proposal shall be reached only after integrating all of the above assessments. In no event shall the financial analysis, by itself, serve as the exclusive basis for such a determination.
(c) Any changes to the proposed financial terms that are accompanied by significant alterations in any of the non-financial factors cited in paragraph (a) shall trigger a re-evaluation of the offer.

Please consider the entire section carefully before answering.
