Instruction: Read the segment of a merger agreement and answer the multiple-choice question by choosing the option that best characterizes the agreement.

Question: Is 'financial point of view' the sole consideration when determining whether a proposal is deemed economically superior?
Option A: No
Option B: Yes

Merger Agreement:
Section 9.5. Superior Proposal Determination: Notwithstanding any other provision of this Agreement, the Company Board, upon receipt of any Acquisition Proposal, shall assess whether such proposal qualifies as a Superior Proposal based on a comprehensive evaluation. This evaluation shall include:
(1) A detailed financial analysis performed in consultation with a nationally recognized financial advisor;
(2) An assessment of non-financial variables, including strategic alignment, market conditions, and competitive positioning, to be reviewed in conjunction with the Company’s external legal counsel;
(3) A mandatory negotiation period of three Business Days during which Parent may propose amendments or counteroffers addressing both financial and strategic aspects of the proposal;
(4) A post-notice review wherein the Board must re-evaluate the proposal’s merits after incorporating feedback from stakeholders and market analysts.
Notably, the financial analysis is merely one component of the evaluation, and the comprehensive assessment explicitly requires the consideration of non-financial factors for the proposal to be affirmed as Superior.

