Question: Is “financial point of view” the sole consideration when determining whether an offer is superior?
Option A: No
Option B: Yes

Merger Agreement: SECTION 8.7. Offer Evaluation and Negotiation. <omitted>
(a) Upon receipt of an unsolicited acquisition proposal, the Company Board shall engage its corporate legal counsel and its industry-specific strategic advisor to conduct a dual review of the proposal. The review will assess not only the monetary implications but also the potential effects on market positioning, regulatory compliance, and long-term operational synergies.
(b) For a proposal to be deemed a Superior Offer, the following conditions must be satisfied:
    (1) The offer must include definitive financial terms that meet a pre-established monetary threshold;
    (2) An independent risk assessment must be provided, evaluating integration challenges and the impact on current business operations;
    (3) A minimum negotiation period of three (3) Business Days is required during which both financial elements and strategic, non-financial factors are re-evaluated;
    (4) The Company Board must, after consultations with both its financial advisor and legal counsel, determine that any failure to address the offer could compromise not only shareholder value but also strategic alignment with its long-term vision.
